2151:     /**
2152:      * This method can be used by cases where the caller has an event that needs to both block for completion but
2153:      * also process background events. For some events, in order to fully process the associated logic, the
2154:      * {@link ConsumerNetworkThread background thread} needs assistance from the application thread to complete.
2155:      * If the application thread simply blocked on the event after submitting it, the processing would deadlock.
2156:      * The logic herein is basically a loop that performs two tasks in each iteration:
2157:      *
2158:      * <ol>
2159:      *     <li>Process background events, if any</li>
2160:      *     <li><em>Briefly</em> wait for {@link CompletableApplicationEvent an event} to complete</li>
2161:      * </ol>
2162:      *
2163:      * <p/>
2164:      *
2165:      * Each iteration gives the application thread an opportunity to process background events, which may be
2166:      * necessary to complete the overall processing.
2167:      *
2168:      * <p/>
2169:      *
2170:      * As an example, take {@link #unsubscribe()}. To start unsubscribing, the application thread enqueues an
2171:      * {@link UnsubscribeEvent} on the application event queue. That event will eventually trigger the
2172:      * rebalancing logic in the background thread. Critically, as part of this rebalancing work, the
2173:      * {@link ConsumerRebalanceListener#onPartitionsRevoked(Collection)} callback needs to be invoked for any
2174:      * partitions the consumer owns. However,
2175:      * this callback must be executed on the application thread. To achieve this, the background thread enqueues a
2176:      * {@link ConsumerRebalanceListenerCallbackNeededEvent} on its background event queue. That event queue is
2177:      * periodically queried by the application thread to see if there's work to be done. When the application thread
2178:      * sees {@link ConsumerRebalanceListenerCallbackNeededEvent}, it is processed, and then a
2179:      * {@link ConsumerRebalanceListenerCallbackCompletedEvent} is then enqueued by the application thread on the
2180:      * application event queue. Moments later, the background thread will see that event, process it, and continue
2181:      * execution of the rebalancing logic. The rebalancing logic cannot complete until the
2182:      * {@link ConsumerRebalanceListener} callback is performed.
2183:      *
2184:      * @param future                    Event that contains a {@link CompletableFuture}; it is on this future that the
2185:      *                                  application thread will wait for completion
2186:      * @param timer                     Overall timer that bounds how long to wait for the event to complete
